    <exercise number="79" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Recall the formula for continually compounding interest, $$y = Ae^{kt}.$$ Use the definition of a logarithm along with properties of logarithms to solve the formula for time $$t$$ such that $$t$$ is equal to a single logarithm.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$t=ln( ( y A ) 1 k ) t=ln( ( y A ) 1 k )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="80" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Recall the compound interest formula $$A = a\left( {1 + \frac{r}{k}} \right)^{kt}.$$ Use the definition of a logarithm along with properties of logarithms to solve the formula for time $$t.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="81" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Newton’s Law of Cooling states that the temperature $$T$$ of an object at any time t can be described by the equation $$T = T_{s} + \left( {T_{0} - T_{s}} \right)e^{- kt},$$ where $$T_{s}$$ is the temperature of the surrounding environment, $$T_{0}$$ is the initial temperature of the object, and $$k$$ is the cooling rate. Use the definition of a logarithm along with properties of logarithms to solve the formula for time $$t$$ such that $$t$$ is equal to a single logarithm.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$t=ln( ( T- T s T 0 - T s ) - 1 k ) t=ln( ( T- T s T 0 - T s ) - 1 k )$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
